igious literature, magazines, and other study materials used by Jehovah's Witnesses worldwide.\n\nThe Watch Tower Society was officially founded in 1881 by Charles Taze Russell, who wanted to create a structured environment for the study of the Bible. The organization has grown tremendously since then and now operates in multiple countries, producing literature in hundreds of languages. The literature published by the Society is considered authoritative by Jehovah's Witnesses and is believed to provide necessary guidance and understanding of the Bible.\n\nIn line with their beliefs, Jehovah's Witnesses rely primarily on Watch Tower Society literature when conducting their meetings. This literature generally includes:\n\n1. The Watchtower: A bi-monthly magazine which serves as the primary source of spiritual guidance and interpretation of biblical teachings for Jehovah's Witnesses. Each issue contains study articles, experiences from fellow Witnesses, and other practical information for members.\n\n2. Awake!: A companion magazine to The Watchtower, focused more on secular issues and topics, encouraging readers to examine matters from a biblical perspective. It generally covers social issues, science, health, and family life.\n\n3. Study publications: Books, brochures, and other publications that are specifically designed for deeper study. These cover a wide range of subjects, such as the Bible's prophecies, creation, and principles for Christian living, and are used in congregation meetings as well as personal study.\n\n4. Meeting workbooks: These are prepared by the Watch Tower Society and outline the weekly meeting schedule, providing specific topics and Bible passages for discussion. Congregation members use these workbooks to prepare for the congregation meetings.\n\n5. Digital resources: The Watch Tower Society also provides additional resources, such as videos, audio files, and electronic publications for use during meetings or personal study through their official website, jw.org.\n\nThe formats and subjects of the meetings held at Kingdom Halls are standardized across the world, which ensures a consistent experience for Jehovah's Witnesses. The Watch Tower Society is central to the religious education and spiritual development of Jehovah's Witnesses, and their literature plays an essential role in connecting individual members with the organization and its teachings. Through systematic study and diligent application of these materials, Jehovah's Witnesses strive to enhance their understanding of the Bible and strengthen their faith."}]}
