iciency of up to 6%, which is higher than the efficiency found in many higher plants.\n\n2. Electric cars: They convert electrical energy to mechanical energy with an efficiency of around 85-90%, making them more energy-efficient than traditional gasoline-powered cars, which have efficiencies around 20-30%.\n\n3. Combined heat and power (CHP) systems: These systems generate electricity and useful thermal energy in a single, integrated system, thus capturing and using the waste heat from electricity generation, reaching efficiencies of up to 80% or more.\n\n4. Gibbons: These arboreal primates can swing through trees at high speeds using relatively little energy due to their specialized anatomy and energy-efficient locomotion technique called brachiation.\n\nIt's important to note that these examples are context-specific, and their energy efficiency may not be universally applicable or comparable across all situations."}]}
