nsion he can't afford). Beano, working in a floral nursery and hounded by the Inland Revenue (the U.K.'s IRS) is the only immediately enthusiastic bandmate. They arrange a meeting, where Karen informs them that she's tracked Brian and found his music royalties are being distributed per a bequest in his will, provoking Beano to compassionately ask if that means \"the silly sod's dead?\" The band again soldiers on. Hughie shows up to complete... My question: what year is does the band strange fruit perform at the wisbech rock festival\nA:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The year the band Strange Fruit performs at the Wisbech Rock Festival is not explicitly mentioned in the provided plot summary. However, since the movie is set in 1998 and revolves around a 20th-anniversary reunion concert, we can presume that their performance at the festival took place around 20 years prior, in the late 1970s."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Answer the following question: Q: What is the term for abnormally low blood levels of carbon dioxide?    Choices:  - spirogyra  - hepatitis  - trichina  - hypocapnia  A:\nAnswer:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The term for abnormally low blood levels of carbon dioxide is hypocapnia.\n\nHypocapnia, also known as low blood CO2 or hypocarbia, refers to a medical condition in which the blood concentration of carbon dioxide (CO2) is diminished. Carbon dioxide is a crucial compound for various physiological processes in the human body. It is primarily produced during the process of cellular respiration, where glucose is broken down into energy. CO2 is transported through the bloodstream and expelled from the body via the lungs when we exhale.\n\nVarious factors can cause hypocapnia. One of the most common is hyperventilation, a condition in which a person breathes too rapidly, causing an excessive amount of CO2 to be expelled from the body. This can be triggered by stress, anxiety, or panic, leading to a temporary reduction in blood CO2 levels. In some cases, hyperventilation can be caused by physiological disorders, such as lung conditions or damage to the central nervous system.\n\nSymptoms of hypocapnia often include dizziness, shortness of breath, lightheadedness, and tingling sensations in the limbs or extremities. In severe cases, hypocapnia can lead to fainting, seizures, or even coma.\n\nTo prevent or treat hypocapnia, patients are often advised to practice slow, deep breathing exercises to help regulate their breathing and ensure adequate CO2 levels in the body. In some cases, medications or other medical interventions may also be necessary, depending on the cause of the hypocapnia.\n\nIt is crucial to note that the other options listed alongside hypocapnia are unrelated to blood levels of carbon dioxide:\n\n- Spirogyra: This is a type of filamentous green algae that forms slimy, green masses in freshwater habitats. It has no connection to blood CO2 levels.\n- Hepatitis: Hepatitis refers to an inflammation of the liver, usually caused by a viral infection. It is completely unrelated to hypocapnia or blood CO2 levels.\n- Trichina: Trichina pertains to a worm of the genus Trichinella, which is a parasite that can cause trichinosis in humans and other animals. The infection occurs after consuming undercooked meat that carries the larval cysts of the parasite. It is not related to blood CO2 levels or hypocapnia."}]}
