ld be one of recovery and that she could have seen the documentary released. \n  \n “Her spirit is really beautifully positive. She’s so open and thoughtful and considerate and honest,” Henze said. “As a documentarian, that’s what you’re always looking for. With Liz, it was not only getting her honest feeling. ... I was brought into her family.” \n  \n Stone called Joice a \"remarkable\" person with a \"sense of love and life.\" Stone recalled visiting Joice after she was released from the ICU and was meeting her newborn for the first time. \n  \n \"She was holding the baby. Just the joy on her face was just incredible,\" said Stone. \"She said ‘This is worth it. ... I would do it all again to have this child.'\" |||||Summary:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Elizabeth Joice was a woman who had cancer but got better after treatment. She was told that she wouldn't be able to have a baby because of the treatment she had. However, she got pregnant and was very happy with her husband, Max. When she was one month pregnant, her cancer came back. She had to make a difficult choice: she could end the pregnancy to get tests to see how bad her cancer was, or she could continue being pregnant without knowing about her cancer. Elizabeth decided to continue the pregnancy and gave birth to a baby girl named Lily. Unfortunately, her cancer got worse and she passed away six weeks after giving birth. Elizabeth's story will be part of a documentary about pregnancy and motherhood."}]}
