oved ones in aviation disasters, boasting a network of 250 grief mentors knowledgeable in air disasters dating back to 1958.\n\n5. The service has proven invaluable for those affected, such as Rachel Courtney, who lost her father in a plane crash in 1998. With the help of a mentor from ACCESS who also lost her father in an air disaster, Courtney was able to cope.\n\n6. ACCESS also serves as a resource for airlines and organizations, offering the necessary information to provide effective emotional support in the aftermath of aviation disasters.\n\n7. Snow authored the book, \"Surviving Sudden Loss: Stories from those who have lived it\", providing another tool for grieving families to navigate through their losses. \n\n8. Each aviation disaster prompts readiness from Snow and her team of grief mentors to extend help to the victims' families, proving essential in their healing process."}]}
