{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Given a definition of a task and a sample input, break the definition into small parts.\nEach of those parts will have some instruction. Explain their meaning by showing an example that meets the criteria in the instruction. Use the following format:\nPart  # : a key part of the definition.\nUsage: Sample response that meets the criteria from the key part. Explain why you think it meets the criteria."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Instructions: Given a real-life anecdote of a complex ethical situation, generate a suitable title that describes the main event/root cause of the situation. Imagine that the given text is a social media post, and you have to write the title of the post, so the users can decide to read the main text or not.\nInput: Hi, so I am 15 years old and for most of my life my parents have constantly argued every single day and they tell each other all the time they want a divorce, they aren’t happy, their a terrible partner, and etc.  I hate hearing them argue out of the blue over pointless things, and most of the time it’s my mom screaming at my dad for unreasonable things and for awhile I never got involved, I would just try to cancel out the noise when I was little. But over the course of a few years the arguing never gets easier to hear, despite my wishes for it to be. So I started to intersect in their arguments which may not be wise because it’s ultimately between them but sometimes it gets too hard to listen to. Anyways, so a couple of years ago I had an argument with my mom that I always tend to think about. One day I found messages that my mom sent another man that were sexual and they were talking about a meet up and etc. This also isn’t the first time I’ve seen questionable messages that she has sent to another man, the first time I was really young maybe around 9 and I brought it up to my dad and my mom told me to tell my dad it was a joke. So anyways that second time I found messages I was really angry and I texted my dad everything and sent him pictures of everything. I was also being impulsive and messaged my mom and told her what I found and how it was stupid of her to waste all of my dad’s time to be with her if she’s in an unhappy relationship. So my mom got home and went apeshit on me and basically was telling me how that’s just how people talk and that my dad and her were fine before I got in the way and started telling him this stuff. So for awhile after that I’ve kept trying to tell my dad to just get a divorce because he’s always complaining about my mom, and I hate that they bring me into their problems and complain about each other to me as it affects me too.  But my dad keeps telling me once he has a stable job he’ll leave (my mom is the breadwinner in my family) and I was recently told my mom would have gotten a divorce with my dad if she wasn’t at risk to lose things that she’s worked hard for. \n\nEvery time I intersect one of their arguments it’s always my mom bringing up stupid little things to my dad and I tend to be very blunt and harsh towards her because what she’s screaming to my dad about is not important. For example, my dad went out for lunch today with his brother (she also went out for lunch yesterday with a friend) and my mom created this huge deal out of it and turned it into she does everything and he does nothing. Which could be farther from the truth, my whole life my dad has supported my brother always trying to make us happy. So every single time my mom brings up a small thing and creates it into an argument I always tell her that she does the exact same thing and she’s being extremely hypocritical for screaming at him for doing something she did yesterday. Every time I tell her how hypocritical and immature she’s being she starts turning it around on me telling me that I’m ungrateful, unappreciative, entitled and etc. Now I feel like I’m always being bitchy to my mom which I hate doing but every time I try to have a conversation with her she turns it into how my dad does nothing everyday. So I keep telling my dad to just leave and I tell my mom to just leave and neither of them do it, despite them always saying they want to. \n\nOverall, basically what I’m trying to get at is that I’ve been trying to get my parents to divorce for a couple years now and I’ve told close friends that I have and they all think I’m fucked up for trying to ruin a marriage, but I honestly believe that it’s been ruined for a while and I just want them to finally get the happiness they want and be with other people.  I love both of my parents I appreciate everything they’ve ever done for me and the support they’ve given me throughout my life. But growing up in this type of household of hearing constant screaming every single day has affected me so much and I just want them to get a divorce and be happy. But I don’t want to rule out other possibilities that I might not understand, for example perhaps they have some sort of arrangement or decision, or maybe they don’t want to get a divorce because I’m still just a kid to them (I’ve brought up that I understand divorce to them and they shouldn’t not get a divorce because of me.) Honestly I wish this wasn’t how I felt about their relationship, I would love to have parents who are together forever, but I want to be realistic about this which is understanding they shouldn’t be together despite my wish for them to be. Anyways, am I the asshole for wanting my parents to divorce? \nOutput:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Part #1: Identify the main event/root cause of the situation.\nUsage: The main event/root cause of this situation is the constant arguing between the parents and the discovery of the mother's questionable messages to another man. This meets the criteria because the arguments and the messages led the child to want their parents to divorce.\n\nPart #2: Consider the ethical complexity of the situation.\nUsage: The ethical complexity comes from