large study shows people with lower blood sugar levels tend to look younger than those with higher blood sugar levels, including diabetics. Alpert and Farris also emphasize that long-term hyperglycemia makes skin more vulnerable and unhealthy, disrupting its natural barrier function.\n\nTo combat the negative effects of sugar on the skin, they recommend a sugar detox plan that begins with a strict three-day diet of no sugar, followed by a gradual reintroduction of certain foods. They suggest consuming foods high in protein, vitamins, and minerals that help repair tissues and boost collagen production, like nuts, omega-3 rich fish, and green tea. In addition to dietary changes, they recommend adopting a skincare regime that includes DIY beauty treatments and topical applications of green tea extract to protect skin from UV-induced inflammation and oxidative stress."}]}
