s now greater than the atmospheric pressure outside the body.\n\n4. This pressure difference forces the carbon dioxide-rich air out of the alveoli and into the bronchioles, then the bronchi, and eventually into the trachea. The trachea is a large tube that connects the lungs to the larynx and pharynx, eventually leading to the oral and nasal cavities.\n\n5. The carbon dioxide-rich air continues to be pushed out of the trachea and passes through the larynx. The larynx, or voice box, is responsible for producing sound, but during exhalation, the vocal cords within the larynx remain open to allow the air to pass through unrestricted.\n\n6. The air then moves through the pharynx, a tube that connects the nasal cavity, oral cavity, and larynx. From the pharynx, the air is expelled out of the body either through the nostrils in the nasal cavity or the mouth in the oral cavity.\n\nIn conclusion, the final step in the respiratory process, after the bloodstream carries carbon dioxide back to the lungs, is the elimination of carbon dioxide from the body through exhalation. This process is crucial in maintaining the balance of gases within the body and ensuring proper cellular function."}]}
