 Church, as it established the College of Cardinals as the exclusive electors of the pope, giving them the power to influence papal succession, and thus, the direction of the church.\n\nThis decision was made in an effort to reduce the influence of external political forces on the process of electing the head of the church and to allow for a more impartial and spiritually guided election. At first, this power was assigned exclusively to the cardinal bishops; however, the Third Lateran Council in 1179 restored this right to the entire body of cardinals, ensuring that the election process would be more inclusive and representative.\n\nIn addition to being granted the privilege of electing the pope, cardinals also played significant roles in the administration of the church of Rome and the papal liturgy. They constituted a privileged class among the Roman clergy and even had the exclusive eligibility of becoming the pope themselves, based on the decree of a synod in 769.\n\nFurthermore, in recognition of their elevated status within the Catholic Church, Pope Innocent IV granted cardinals the privilege of wearing the red hat in 1244, symbolizing the blood they would be willing to shed for the church. The tradition of the red hat continues to be a characteristic element of cardinal attire today.\n\nIn summary, the Papal Bull \"In nomine Domini\" issued during the pontificate of Pope Nicholas II in 1059 conferred the right to elect the pope upon the cardinals, solidifying their central role in determining the leadership and direction of the Catholic Church."}]}
