ions opposed the partition and surrounding Arab states subsequently attacked the newly formed state of Israel in the 1948 Arab-Israeli War.\n\nThroughout the years, various initiatives, resolutions, and peace processes have attempted to address the status of Jerusalem. For instance, the 1993 Oslo Accords suggested reevaluating Jerusalem's status during permanent status negotiations between Israel and the Palestine Liberation Organization. While there have been proposals recommending putting Jerusalem under international control or designating it as the capital for both Israel and Palestine, no consensus has been reached thus far.\n\nIn the current state of affairs, Israel considers Jerusalem as its undivided capital, and it exercises full control over the city, including the contentious Eastern part, which was occupied during the 1967 Six-Day War. Meanwhile, Palestinians claim East Jerusalem as the capital of their future state. The international community has not reached a unified stance, with some countries recognizing Jerusalem as Israel's capital, others supporting the Palestinian claim, and many more advocating for a negotiated solution to the conflict.\n\nIn conclusion, while the idea of placing Jerusalem under international protection has been proposed due to the ongoing disputes, it is not possible to tell that Jerusalem is under international protection because many groups fight over it. The city's status remains contested, and negotiations between Israel and Palestine will likely continue to determine the future administration of Jerusalem."}]}
