entre staff.\""}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Former Work and Pensions Secretary Iain Duncan Smith is demanding that UK Prime Minister Theresa May reverses cuts made to Universal Credit, a single-payment welfare system that began implementation under then-Prime Minister David Cameron. Introduced in order to encourage people to move from benefits back into work, the system sees six benefits disbursed in a single payment, including Jobseeker's Allowance and Employment and Support Allowance. Duncan Smith also argues that the cuts to work allowance cost people up to £1,000 as they attempt to move off benefits into work."}]}
